A||||4||WisconsinGT||Wiper Arms ||||1||
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||09-09-2000||06:55 PM||||Can anyone tell me how to remove them? I did try to pry them off as mentioned in the Hayes manual, but no luck. Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||09-09-2000||07:57 PM||||The manual shows a special tool to use...however everwhere Ive been theyve said "Ive never heard of that before"...whatever.<BR>Anyway, get a flat head screwdriver and use it to lift up on the tab thats on the end of the arm...its on the "drivers" side of the wiper arm where it slides over the round part (where its connected to the car)...<BR>You'll need something to put under the screwdriver.....hard to explain. Think of a teeter totter...put something under the screwdriver in the middle of it, that way you can push DOWN on the s-driver handle so that the blade lifts UP on the tab..when youve got the tab lifted, pull the arm off.<BR>Its kind of an art..but you'll get it. Good luck!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Brian||09-09-2000||09:25 PM||||I recently solved the very same problem<BR>with a bit more brute force. I new of the tab<BR>but even with an 18" screwdriver I still coulden't get the arm to budge. It was so bad I started to chew up the plastic shroud around the post from my efforts.<BR>My soution was to get piece of 1/4" steel,<BR>cut a half round in one end & then bend that end 90 degrees. The end result was much like a pickle fork with a bend in the end.<BR>Slip that under the post end of the arm, cushion the pivot point with a bit of 1/4"<BR>plywood & pry.<BR>Worked the first time out. <BR>Once the arm was off I saw why it was so stubborn. They couldn't have ever been removed in the cars lifetime. The post and the arm were so badly corroded there was no way it was gonna be removed gently.<P>good luck,<BR>Brian||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||09-09-2000||09:40 PM||||If you look closely at the base of the arm you will see a little hole - raise the arm and put a finishing nail in the hole to lock the arm is a raised position. The arm then can easily be pulled off the stud.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-09-2000||11:50 PM||||What Phil said......<P>The nail trick works mosk of the time unless the inside of the arm is really corroded. Try rocking the arm a bit as you pull it off. <P>Just in case you do need it. The tool is available from <A HREF="http://www.lislecorp.com/" TARGET=_blank>Lisle</A>. It can be ordered thru anyplace that carries Lisle tools.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-09-2000).]||||reg||1
